lic  inventories that I saw for curiosity  cabinets included descriptions of Flay  tattooed skins of native people  skeletons pipes wampum giving me an  inkling of what I might find however  experiencing the conflict of moving a  native burial to a more convenient spot  during Reconstruction in Massachusetts  or viewing a Maori human head on display  abroad were deeply disturbing yet I am  compelled to be present and witnessed by  a long line of my female Wampanoag  ancestors in traditional native society  women use their unique perspective to  shape society as defined in the word for  woman mattel misis in Wampanoag it means  the one who has the final say unlike the  clan mothers of old we do not have all  the ancestor all the answers excuse me  my nation is confronted by the results  of looting on a massive scale over  centuries and spanning the globe our  free prior informed consent was neither  sought nor given in this matter nowhere  does stealing from Graves buying human  remains or conducting DNA analysis on  our ancestors fit into our traditional  wampa nog ways of being and doing in the  early contact period and when I say that  I know that's really subject to  interpretation but early contact I think  in this context it's about 16th century  maybe early 17th century in the north  east coast so in the early contact  period a native person admitted to being  the one who pointed out a tribal  cemetery after it had been looted by the  newcomers that person was put to death  by the tribe the sachem ticket talbot  lodged formal complaints with the 17th  century Massachusetts Bay Colony after a  great bear skin and other gifts were  stolen from his mother's grave so tribal  protests were not known or tribal  protocols give me were not known or  respected by the outside world today  there is still little thought given to  tribal credos for honoring those who  have passed away we come together in the  aftermath to think of new ways to  properly return return the dead to  mother earth in humility we ask for  guidance on restoring their repose the  way the Creator intended to facilitate a  lasting peace I have gained valuable  insights through contact with non native  peoples who evidence different  files of communication have other  methods of assigning value and give  different weights to native men's and  women's knowledge and responsibilities  as compared to my own Eastern tribal  network on Turtle Island I do think with  more exposure to Native Americans that  the global consciousness is evolving to  more fully include the federal tribal  voice in authenticating our items and  determining the most ethical fate for  sensitive materials the Aquinnah tribe  recognizes the need for identifying and  developing positive professional  relationships with international  repositories facilitated through the  creation of mechanisms including  international laws and memorandums of  agreement a key component in making  these successful is developing an  ability to practice cultural sensitivity  to our tribal beliefs in dealing with  the sacred an employee of the Aquinnah  Wampanoag Historic Preservation Office I  met with honor Keeler about five years  ago at the Association on American  Indian Affairs and her ovid at her  office in Providence Rhode Island we  have benefited from joining the working  group conference calls members of the  tipo department from aquinnah have  contributed insights from a native  perspective providing thank you  providing colonial military context for  the acquisition of Wampanoag remains  funeral objects and ceremonial items  from our Massachusetts and Rhode Island  homelands my tribe received assistance  in tracking wampa nog objects we  attended the AAA IAS first annual  conference on international repatriation  hosted by the Chickasaw Nation and  additionally benefited from consulting  with the working group on international  repatriation about applicable laws and  to hear about precedent set by other 